# Mock Server Consistency Pass (D1.11 companion)

## What this is

A self-consistency check across the entire Developer 1 retailer flow —
login → create delivery → dashboard list → delivery detail — run
against one coherent in-memory mock server, so a created delivery
genuinely has to reappear correctly in later fetches within the same
run, not just satisfy isolated fixture responses.

## What this proves

That the request/response assumptions documented across `AuthContext.jsx`,
`RetailerDashboard.jsx`, `DeliveryCreate.jsx`, and `RetailerDeliveryDetail.jsx`
are internally consistent with each other, and that the actual
`src/lib/api.js` logic (not a hand-wavy reimplementation — verified
programmatically by `verify-sync.mjs`) behaves correctly against a
backend matching those assumptions.

## What this does NOT prove

That Developer 2's **real** backend matches these assumptions. This is
a mock, built from what Developer 1 has documented as an assumption at
every step (see the `ASSUMED request/response shape` comments
throughout the frontend source) — not Developer 2's actual
implementation, which Claude has no access to. For that, see the
companion deliverable: `manual-test-checklist.zip`.

## Running it yourself

```
node verify-sync.mjs          # confirms api-client.mjs matches the real src/lib/api.js
node mock-server.mjs &        # starts the mock backend on :4400
node run-consistency-test.mjs # runs the full flow, 18 checks
```

## Files

- `reference-api.js` — snapshot of the real `src/lib/api.js` as of D1.10, for the sync check
- `api-client.mjs` — Node-runnable copy of the same logic (only the base-URL line differs, since `import.meta.env` doesn't exist in plain Node)
- `verify-sync.mjs` — proves the above two are functionally identical
- `mock-server.mjs` — coherent in-memory mock backend (login, create, list, detail)
- `run-consistency-test.mjs` — the actual flow test, 18 assertions
